WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.720
In Mozambique's Gorongosa National Park, soldiers on both sides of a civil war

2
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.160
killed elephants for ivory.

3
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.560
The war lasted from 1977 to 1992.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.320
Before the war, about 1 in 5 sampled females had no tusks.

5
00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.920
Among the females that survived, about half had none.

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.700
The rise did not come from elephants losing their tusks.

7
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.060
So how did the population change?

8
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.780
The answer starts with a fact.

9
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.180
Individuals differ.

10
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.380
Variation means the differences among individuals in a population.

11
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.160
An allele is one version of a gene.

12
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.680
A mutation is a change in DNA.

13
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.720
It is the original source of new alleles.

14
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.420
Gene flow is the movement of alleles from one population to another.

15
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.000
Sexual reproduction shuffles alleles that already exist into new combinations.

16
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:02.013
Populations tend to make more young than the environment can support.

17
00:01:02.853 --> 00:01:09.173
A female blue crab in the Chesapeake Bay may carry an egg mass of about 750,000 to 8

18
00:01:09.173 --> 00:01:13.073
million eggs. Only a small fraction become adult crabs.

19
00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:17.953
Most die young. An elephant usually has one calf at a birth.

20
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:20.093
Now add a pressure.

21
00:01:20.973 --> 00:01:25.333
A selective pressure is anything in the environment that makes some variants more

22
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:28.273
likely to survive or reproduce than others.

23
00:01:29.213 --> 00:01:31.353
In Gorongosa, poachers wanted ivory.

24
00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:34.273
So tusked elephants were killed more often.

25
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:38.773
Tuskless females already existed, and they survived more often.

26
00:01:39.493 --> 00:01:40.873
That is natural selection.

27
00:01:41.853 --> 00:01:46.433
Individuals with certain inherited variations survive and reproduce more than

28
00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:47.433
others.

29
00:01:47.717 --> 00:01:49.117
Now count the survivors.

30
00:01:50.037 --> 00:01:52.397
The share of females with no tusks went up.

31
00:01:53.217 --> 00:01:55.857
Tuskless survivors left more of the next generation.

32
00:01:56.637 --> 00:02:00.877
So that inherited variation becomes more common in later generations.

33
00:02:01.757 --> 00:02:03.337
No single elephant changed.

34
00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:05.337
The population changed.

35
00:02:06.577 --> 00:02:07.577
Individuals do not evolve.

36
00:02:08.497 --> 00:02:09.497
Populations do.

37
00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:11.527
Back to the question.

38
00:02:12.447 --> 00:02:16.067
Why do some individuals survive and reproduce while others do not?

39
00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:20.367
Because they differ, and some variations suit the conditions better.

40
00:02:21.247 --> 00:02:22.947
Those individuals leave more young.

41
00:02:23.647 --> 00:02:25.787
So those variations become more common.

42
00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:30.807
A strong pressure, like heavy poaching, can change a trait within a few decades.

43
00:02:31.747 --> 00:02:33.247
A weak pressure changes it slowly.

44
00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:35.267
Remember this rule.

45
00:02:36.187 --> 00:02:39.507
Individuals differ, some survive more, and the population changes.

46
00:02:39.507 --> 00:02:40.187
For more information visit www .fema .gov
